  "loss function cost function mse mean squared error cross entropy log loss mae huber maximum likelihood estimation mle objective",
  "A loss function measures how wrong a model's predictions are, and training means finding parameters that minimise it. Many common losses come from maximum likelihood.",
  "Mean squared error (MSE) penalises large errors heavily and suits regression with roughly normal errors. Mean absolute error (MAE) is more robust to outliers, and Huber loss blends the two. Cross-entropy, or log loss, is used for classification and punishes confident wrong probabilities severely. Maximum likelihood estimation chooses parameters that make the observed data most probable: assuming normal errors gives MSE, and assuming a Bernoulli outcome gives cross-entropy. The loss you train on should match what the business cares about, or at least be closely related.",
