  "linear regression ordinary least squares ols coefficients intercept r squared residuals multicollinearity assumptions ridge lasso regularisation",
  "Linear regression predicts a number as a weighted sum of features, and its coefficients show how the prediction changes with each feature.",
  "Ordinary least squares finds the coefficients that minimise squared errors. Each coefficient is the expected change in the target for a one-unit change in that feature, holding the others fixed. R-squared is the share of variance explained. The assumptions are a linear relationship, independent errors with constant variance, and little multicollinearity, which makes coefficients unstable. Check residual plots for patterns. Ridge (L2) and lasso (L1) regularisation shrink coefficients to reduce overfitting, and lasso can set some to zero. Coefficients describe association, not causation, unless the design supports it.",
  "A delivery time model gives 0.8 minutes per km and 6 minutes for rain: a 10 km delivery in rain is predicted to take 8 + 6 = 14 minutes more than a nearby dry one, plus the intercept.",
